# Every text file is LF in the index and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` says locally.
#
# This used to read `* text=lf`, which does nothing at all: `text` is a boolean
# (or `auto`), so git reads any other value as "unspecified" and falls back to
# `core.autocrlf` -- which Git for Windows installs as `true`. So Windows
# checkouts got CRLF, and a test that writes a fixture from a `.feature`
# docstring and then asserts a line number in the output got its lines counted
# twice: behave keeps the CR on each docstring line, the fixture step writes it
# through a text-mode handle (CR LF -> CR CR LF), and reading that back with
# universal newlines turns one line break into two. That is what put the four
# `features/lint.feature` ASSY findings a line late on windows-latest, and only
# there.
#
# `text=auto` rather than a bare `text`: `*` covers the binaries too, and there
# are some no rule below claims -- `examples/**/cube.3mf` (a zip container) and
# `examples/feature_render_custom/bracket.pdf`. A bare `text` would declare
# those to be text and rewrite their bytes; `auto` sniffs the content and leaves
# anything binary alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.step binary
*.stl binary
**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The dev container's polyglot initializeCommand shim: cmd.exe reads the top of it
# and /bin/sh the bottom, and the shell half breaks on a trailing CR -- so pin it to
# LF whatever the checkout's core.eol says.
/.devcontainer/docker-socket-init.cmd text eol=lf
